improve the text:  Recalling the solution of the initial value problem \eq{iv} of the classical harmonic oscillator, in order to introduce time into the associated Schrödinger equation, we are induced to make the loose connection
\begin{align}
x(t) &= a\cos\omega t=a\,\text{Re}(e^{i\omega t})\sim \phi(x)\text{Re}\left(e^{i\frac{E}{\hbar} t}\right)\!.
\end{align}
In this first step it seems natural to replace the amplitude $a$ with the solution $\phi(x)$ of the time-independent equation, where $x$ can be a generic amplitude. After we have used the correspondence between classical and quantum frequencies.
To introduce time into the wave function, we can express it as the product of two functions, one of position and the other of time: $\psi(x,t)=\phi(x)f(t)$. Since $f(t)=\text{Re}\left(e^{i\frac{E}{\hbar} t}\right)$ is the sum of two functions, we can opt to make the following choice
\begin{align}
\psi(x,t)=\phi(x)e^{-i\frac{E}{\hbar} t},
\end{align}
where the complex exponential $e^{-i\frac{E}{\hbar} t}$ is a phasor, i.e. a unit vector that rotates clockwise in the complex plane at a frequency $\omega$.
Then we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}=-i\frac{E}{\hbar}\psi(x,t) \Rightarrow E\psi=i\hbar\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}.
\end{align*}
It follows that, from $\hat{H}\phi=E\phi$, we get the time-dependent equation
\begin{align}\label{td}
\hat{H}\psi=i\hbar\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}.
\end{align}
So the Hamiltonian governs the evolution in time. 
An immediate argument for the correctness of this equation is that it supports the probabilistic interpretation of wave functions, i.e., the fact that the probability $P=\int\psi\psi^*$ is independent of time, which means that quantum objects cannot disappear.
In fact, from \q{td} and its complex conjugate we get
\begin{align}\nonumber
&\frac{d}{dt}\int\psi\psi^*dx= \int(\psi^*\partial_t\psi+\psi\,\partial_t\psi^*)\,dx\\
&=\frac{i}{\hbar}\left(\int\!\psi(\hat{H}\psi)^*dx-\!\int\!\psi^*\hat{H}\psi\,dx\right)\!dx=0,
\end{align}
because $\hat{H}$ is hermitian. 
As is common in generalizations, we assume that the time-dependent 1D Schrödinger equation holds not only when $\psi(x,t)$ can't be factorized, but even in the presence of time-dependent potentials in the Hamiltonian.
